---
// Performance measure setup — one page per measure.
//
// WHAT THIS PAGE IS FOR. Deciding what a number will be able to tell you, and
// what you will make people do to get it. Sort the fields by what survives being
// wrong:
//
//   - name, program, decimals, definition prose, guidance — edit any of them a
//     year in and nothing breaks. Fill-in-the-blank.
//   - the COUNTING RULE — change it and every historical total silently changes
//     meaning. "Sum" is effort delivered; "Union" is ground in a condition. Same
//     arithmetic, opposite claims.
//   - the DIMENSIONS — change them and you have a hole. A reported dimension
//     cannot be backfilled: entries filed before you added it never asked the
//     question.
//
// So the irreversible decisions get the room, and everything recoverable gets a
// band it can be scanned in without opening.
//
// THE PAGE IS FOUR ROLE ZONES, because the fields have four different jobs and
// a layout that files them together files them wrongly:
//
//   1. METADATA (the band) — name, program, definition, guidance. Set once.
//   2. WHAT EACH ENTRY RECORDS (firma2-measure-record) — the primary category
//      plus the amount and the counting rule. The KPI block being built.
//
// THE BAND IS PINNED IN THE RAIL, flat rather than collapsed; both working
// sections are in the scrolling column. The split is availability against room:
// metadata is set once and never worth navigating back to, so it stays in view;
// the decisions — what this measures, what else is recorded about it — are what
// an author is actually working on, and they get the wide column. The record
// tested that line by spending a release in the rail and coming back: it holds
// the counting rule, and the brief says irreversible decisions get the room.
//   3. ALSO ASKED OF THE REPORTER (firma2-reporter-questions) — supporting
//      questions, each a real cost on every entry forever.
//   4. FILLED IN AUTOMATICALLY (firma2-auto-breakdowns) — derived breakdowns,
//      free, answered by a layer, the record, or history.
//
// THE SHAPE, AND WHY IT HAS CHANGED FOUR TIMES. A four-step wizard, then four
// full-width form cards, then a live preview beside a dense config rail, then a
// definition band over one flat "dimensions" list — and now the four zones.
// Each change fixed a real fault worth remembering:
//
//   1. A wizard assumes the user finishes in one sitting. Defining a measure is
//      part exploration and part consulting other people, so the record is
//      created by answering ONE question (output or outcome) and lives at a URL
//      you can bookmark and send to a colleague.
//   2. Four cards of empty inputs made the DEFINITION the hero. It isn't — an
//      input renders the same whether it holds an answer or nothing, so a
//      measure eight-tenths finished looked exactly like one not started.
//   3. The config rail put the one deliberative task — declaring the question
//      set — into a 22rem column, beside previews that had the wide one. A
//      preview only earns its width beside the lever that moves it.
//   4. The flat list fixed the width and kept the jumble: the primary category
//      — the single most defining choice on the page — was a radio decorating
//      one row among six, and the amount fields sat in the band next to a
//      precision picker. Fields with four different roles rendered as two
//      undifferentiated containers. The zones are those roles, made layout.
//
// WHAT THE PREVIEWS ANSWER, and neither is a report — nothing here has been
// reported, this is a definition that does not exist yet:
//   - The ENTRY FORM PREVIEW is the COST — the form a reporter meets.
//   - The BREAKDOWN PREVIEW is the PAYOFF, one breakdown drawn as a split so a
//     list of vocabularies stops being abstract.
// They close the column TOGETHER, in that order. Cost then payoff is the trade
// this page asks an author to make, and split across two columns — which is
// where they were — the pair was never actually seen as a pair.
//
// An earlier version of the entry-form panel drew every subcategory combination
// as a dense matrix and warned "16 values per reporting period". That was wrong
// in the damaging direction — reporting is sparse rows, not a filled grid — so
// it made a cheap feature look punishing. Every panel now describes the
// definition rather than mocking up a form nobody fills that way.
//
// All three degrade rather than block on an incomplete draft, which is the state
// this page is in most often.
//
// ROUTE. getStaticPaths builds one page per measure from `measures`, keyed by
// the slug already on the record — the same slug measureHref() builds links
// from, so the catalog can never point at a page this route did not generate.
//
// ZERO PAGE CSS. The inline custom properties below are SPINE knobs, not styles:
// the layout primitive's own documented hooks, set where the layout is declared.
// Every section owns its own markup and CSS.
import AppLayout from '../../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Firma2PageHeader from '../../../components/shared/firma2-page-header.astro';
import Firma2RecordRail from '../../../components/shared/firma2-record-rail.astro';
import EsaPill from '@esa/ecology/esa-pill.astro';
import Firma2MeasureDefinition from '../../../components/measures/firma2-measure-definition.astro';
import Firma2MeasureRecord from '../../../components/setup/firma2-measure-record.astro';
import Firma2MeasureAttributes from '../../../components/setup/firma2-measure-attributes.astro';
import Firma2MeasureReporterPreview from '../../../components/measures/firma2-measure-reporter-preview.astro';
import Firma2MeasureSplitChart from '../../../components/measures/firma2-measure-split-chart.astro';
import {
  measures,
  measureDisplayName,
  outstandingFields,
  outstandingLine,
  MEASURE_STATUS_TONE,
} from '../../../data/firma2-performance-measures';
import type { PerformanceMeasureDefinition } from '../../../data/firma2-performance-measures';
import { withBase } from '../../../lib/base';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return measures.map((measure) => ({
    params: { measure: measure.slug },
    props: { measure },
  }));
}

interface Props {
  measure: PerformanceMeasureDefinition;
}

const { measure } = Astro.props;

// A measure reaches this page before it has a name, so the title, the crumb and
// the document title all route through one fallback rather than each deciding
// separately what to do about an empty string.
const displayName = measureDisplayName(measure);
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: sidebar[data-side=end, 28rem] > [ rail · stack(2xl) ]
  sections:
    - rail (100dvh, sticky) -> firma2-record-rail
      - metadata band          -> firma2-measure-definition  (flat, not a <details>)
    - page header           -> firma2-page-header      (sticky band)
    - the record            -> firma2-measure-record       "What this measures"
    - other subcategories   -> firma2-measure-attributes   (reported + derived, one list)
    - entry form preview    -> firma2-measure-reporter-preview   (the COST)
    - breakdown preview     -> firma2-measure-split-chart        (the PAYOFF)
    - autosave failure      -> esa-snackbar-container  (silent on success)
  note: the rail holds the set-once identity facts, in view for the whole page;
        the column runs the decisions — what each entry records, what else is
        asked, what arrives free — then the two previews, read as a pair.
-->
<AppLayout title={`${displayName} — ProjectFirma 2.0`} active="performance-measures">
  <!-- data-firma2-measure-root marks the RECORD'S BOUNDARY. Every control that
       edits this measure is inside it and nothing in the app shell is, which is
       what the controller delegates its listeners to — see the note in
       src/lib/measure-editor.ts. It is on the wrapper rather than on any one
       section precisely so a control can move between sections without the
       previews going quiet. -->
  <div
    class="sidebar"
    data-side="end"
    data-firma2-measure-root
    style="--sidebar-width: 28rem; --sidebar-content-min: 34rem"
  >
    <!-- THE RAIL HOLDS THE BLOCK BEING BUILT. Everything else on this page is
         declared AGAINST what an entry records: a supporting question is a
         question asked after the primary category, a derived breakdown is a cut
         of the amount, and both previews are renders of the whole thing. Until
         now that block was zone 2 of a scrolling column, so by the time an author
         was declaring their third supporting question the category it supports
         had been off screen for a screen and a half.
         Pinned, it is the one thing on the page that is always both visible and
         editable — which is what firma2-record-rail is for, and the same
         component the project detail page uses on the same edge.

         THE TENSION, STATED: the counting rule is the most consequential setting
         on this screen ("1,240 acres" is ambiguous between effort delivered and
         ground in a condition until it is answered), and this brief says
         irreversible decisions get the ROOM. A 28rem rail is less room than the
         wide column it had. What it buys instead is permanence — the setting is
         never more than a glance away and never needs to be navigated back to —
         and the rule keeps its full swapping help sentence, which is where the
         weight actually lives. 28rem rather than the project page's 25rem
         precisely so that sentence still reads as a sentence.

         --sidebar-content-min AS A LENGTH, unchanged in reasoning from the inner
         sidebar this replaces: the primitive's default is 60%, a ratio of a
         container whose width already depends on the app nav's collapse state.
         34rem ties the wrap point to what the centre actually needs — the
         question rows go two-column at the switcher's 32rem — instead of to a
         proportion. -->
    <Firma2RecordRail>
      <!-- IDENTITY FIRST, THEN WHAT IT COLLECTS. The band states what kind of
           measure this is and where it rolls up ("Output measure · Forest Health
           & Fuels") in one line, and opens to the four set-once fields behind
           it. Reading down the rail is then the record in the order it was
           decided: what this measure IS, then what each entry records.

           IT CAME OUT OF THE MAIN COLUMN, where it was the first thing under the
           page header and the only band on the page that was not a question the
           author was answering. Two reasons it is better here. It is METADATA —
           set once at creation, rarely touched — so it was spending the widest,
           highest-value strip of the page on the content least likely to be
           edited; and it belongs to the same object as the record below it, so
           splitting the two across a column boundary made one record read as
           two sections.

           IT NEEDS NO NARROWING. Both of its inner layouts are `.switcher` at a
           40rem threshold, so at the rail's 28rem they stack to one column on
           their own. The band was already written to survive a narrow container;
           nothing here is a special case for the rail. -->
      <Firma2MeasureDefinition measure={measure} />
    </Firma2RecordRail>

    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xl">
    <!-- Two crumbs, not three: the sidenav already owns the way home, and the one
         thing a reader needs from here is the way back to the catalog. Crumb and
         title ride in the sticky band, so that way back stays on screen for the
         whole page rather than only its first screenful. -->
    <Firma2PageHeader
      title={displayName}
      crumbs={[
        { label: 'Performance measures', href: withBase('/prototypes/performance-measures') },
        { label: displayName },
      ]}
    >
      <!-- Status rides in the actions cluster because esa-page-header has no
           status slot — the gap the project detail route already documents. It
           is not an action: no href, no handler, not focusable. The
           visually-hidden "Status" is load-bearing, since a pill reading
           "Draft" is unambiguous beside a title only because of where it sits.
           The config rail deliberately does NOT repeat it. -->
      <!-- READINESS, BESIDE THE STATUS IT EXPLAINS. The whole-measure count used
           to ride on the definition band, where it claimed fields living two
           zones below. It then moved here to sit beside Save, on the argument
           that an unfinished measure is what Save is blocked by — and it stays
           here now that Save has gone, because the better reason was always the
           one next to it: this line is why the pill says Draft. Count and status
           are one fact said twice, at two levels of detail, and they belong
           together. Non-focusable text on the status pill's own precedent,
           server-rendered from the seed and kept live by measure-editor.ts
           through the same outstandingLine() — one copy of the sentence,
           imported by both. -->
      <span id="fmc-readiness" slot="actions" class="typography-label-md"
        >{outstandingLine(outstandingFields(measure).length)}</span
      >

      <span slot="actions" class="visually-hidden">Status</span>
      <EsaPill
        slot="actions"
        label={measure.status}
        variant={MEASURE_STATUS_TONE[measure.status]}
        size="md"
        removable={false}
      />

      <!-- NO SAVE, NO CANCEL. There were both, and removing them is not a
           subtraction of function — the draft still persists, it just persists
           without being asked. measure-editor.ts already listened to every
           control on the record to keep the previews live; it now writes the
           draft on that same signal, so a change is saved the moment it is made.

           WHY THE BUTTONS WERE THE ODD ONES OUT. This spoke's editing model is
           stated in firma2-editable-field: "there are no Save or Cancel buttons
           and no pencil — the field's edge is its own boundary, and crossing it
           is the save gesture." Every field on the project detail page works
           that way. This page shipped a form-style commit bar on top of the same
           idea, which asked the author to hold two mental models on one product:
           correct a project by leaving the field, but correct a measure by
           leaving the field AND then finding a button.

           IT ALSO SUITED THIS SCREEN WORST. The whole argument of this page is
           that a measure is defined over days, from a bookmarked URL, in
           consultation with other people — and a commit bar is the one control
           that punishes leaving. Autosave is what the model already claimed.

           WHAT WAS ACTUALLY LOST: Cancel discarded everything since the last
           save by reloading. There is no bulk undo now. That is a real
           reduction and the honest one to note; per-field abandon is what the
           project pages give (Escape) and what this page's raw controls do not
           yet have. Worth building if authors miss it. -->
    </Firma2PageHeader>

    <!-- THE TWO WORKING SECTIONS, then the two previews. What is NOT here is
         the metadata band, pinned in the rail — the argument is in the rail
         block at the top of this file. What is left is a column that reads as
         one activity: every decision an author makes about this measure, in the
         order they make them, followed by what those decisions cost and what
         they buy.

         EACH ZONE RECEIVES THE SAME dimensions ARRAY and takes its own slice
         (the rail's record takes the primary, questions the supporting reported,
         breakdowns the derived). The page deliberately does not pre-slice: the
         zones' own filters are the single statement of which rows belong where,
         and measure-editor.ts recomposes the array from the same three slices.

         THE ENTRY FORM PREVIEW CAME OUT OF THE RAIL and now closes the column
         beside the breakdown preview. It is a better place than the swap made it
         look: the two previews are the COST and the PAYOFF of one definition —
         the form a reporter meets, and the chart the author gets for making them
         meet it — and reading them as a pair is the trade this page is asking
         the author to make. Split across two columns they were never seen
         together.

         It also retires a documented divergence. The old arrangement put the
         preview FIRST in the DOM and second on screen (`data-side="end"` on the
         inner sidebar), so reading order and visual order disagreed under
         SC 1.3.2. In one column there is nothing to reconcile. -->
    <!-- ZONE 2 IS BACK IN THE COLUMN. It spent a release in the rail on the
         argument that everything below is declared against it — true, and not
         enough: this is the one zone on the page where an author is making the
         DECISIONS the whole measure turns on, and the counting rule in
         particular is the setting that decides whether "1,240 acres" means
         effort delivered or ground in a condition. The brief says irreversible
         decisions get the room. A 28rem rail is not the room.
         What stayed in the rail is the metadata band, which wants the opposite
         treatment: set once, rarely touched, and useful to have in view the
         whole time. Availability for the facts, room for the decisions. -->
    <Firma2MeasureRecord measure={measure} />

    <!-- ONE LIST OF SUBCATEGORIES, NOT TWO SECTIONS. "Also asked of the
         reporter" and "Filled in automatically" merged into this, and the merge
         is the page finally matching its own data model: a measure has the
         thing it measures (above, with the primary subcategory inside it) and
         then attributes on every entry — and whether an attribute is answered
         by the reporter or derived from a layer is a property of the row, not a
         different species of thing. The cost signal the two-section split used
         to carry moved to where it operates: the fork between the two add
         affordances, and the source marker on every row. The component's own
         header holds the full argument. -->
    <Firma2MeasureAttributes dimensions={measure.dimensions} />
    <Firma2MeasureReporterPreview measure={measure} />
    <Firma2MeasureSplitChart measure={measure} />

    <!-- Autosave failure only — see the note in src/lib/measure-editor.ts. No
         `duration`: the lego documents auto-dismiss as an SC 2.2.1 (Timing
         Adjustable) failure unless the host supplies its own adjustment, and
         persistent-until-dismissed is the conforming default. -->
    <esa-snackbar-container></esa-snackbar-container>
    </div>
  </div>
</AppLayout>

<script>
  // WIRING, not a section. The band and the three zones own the controls and
  // the previews own their own rendering; none of them can own the record they
  // share, so the page holds the controller that reads the controls, layers
  // local drafts, and announces the working record. Everything it does lives in
  // the module — this block exists only because the page is the one place that
  // knows the seed record and that every section is present.
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-snackbar-container';
  import { initMeasureEditor } from '../../../lib/measure-editor';
  import { measures } from '../../../data/firma2-performance-measures';

  // The slug is read from the DOM rather than interpolated into the script,
  // because a define:vars script cannot use import statements. One selector, not
  // two: the `[data-firma2-rollup-slug]` fallback pointed at the roll-up panel,
  // which no longer exists, so it could only ever return null.
  const slug = document
    .querySelector('[data-firma2-reporter-preview]')
    ?.getAttribute('data-firma2-reporter-preview');
  const seed = measures.find((m) => m.slug === slug);
  if (seed) initMeasureEditor({ seed });
</script>
